From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] nx2: an eviction whose write-back scratch cannot grow keeps the image (waveydude's 0913, EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM), off by default

What: the texture collector writes a GPU-written image back to guest memory before it deletes it.
A block-linear write-back reads each guest level into the swizzle scratch (SwizzleImage), and the
scratch grows to the level's size there, after the staging copy is made, in the middle of the
write-back. waveydude's crash 20261111-005114 was that growth: a refused 32 MiB allocation during
texture write-back.

With EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM=1 (exact "1", read once; Common::PeSwitch) the scratch is grown to
the image's largest level first, before a staging buffer is taken or a guest byte written: in the
collector's Cleanup (right before the write-back, so also before the Vulkan port's prefetched copy is
used) and in the refusal eviction's batch (EDEN_NX2_REFUSAL_EVICT). A refused growth (bad_alloc)
keeps the image as it is, GPU-written, tracked and registered, for a later pass; Cleanup counts it in
gc_downloads_deferred, the batch stops there as it does for a refused staging buffer. One line per
refusal on the C stream stderr: "EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM scratch_bytes=<n>".

Changed from his: the growth is asked for only when WritebackNeedsHeap (our 0565/GC_WRITEBACK_SKIP
predictor, util.cpp) says the write-back would grow the scratch by a large request. His version
grew it for every block-linear image, also one whose guest range is a single host span (no scratch
used) or one the write-back bands cover: up to 32 MiB asked for at the moment memory is short, for
nothing. The image and the write-back are his otherwise.

Without 0663 (EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM's second half) a refused growth still goes through the
operator new handler first (EDEN_UP_DOOR_WAIT, EDEN_UP_NEW_WAIT_MS waits, the EDEN_HEAP_FAILURE line,
a heap snapshot) and is then caught here instead of ending the session.

Off: one test of a static bool per dirty eviction, then the old lines; nothing allocated, nothing
logged. Not in the kept-store stamp (TranslationSwitches): it changes no translation.

Origin and credit: waveydude, patch 0913 ("Defer texture eviction if writeback scratch cannot
grow"), commit 4c2e760.
---
diff --git a/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h b/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
index 1e16e2d..d5210db 100644
--- a/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
+++ b/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
@@ -506,6 +506,36 @@ TextureCache<P>::TextureCache(Runtime& runtime_, Tegra::MaxwellDeviceMemoryManag
     }
 }
 
+// EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM=1 (exact "1"; waveydude's 0913): a block-linear write-back needs
+// scratch for one guest level at a time (SwizzleImage). When it would grow the scratch by a large
+// request (WritebackNeedsHeap: not a host span, not covered by the write-back bands, 256 KiB or
+// more past the capacity), the scratch is grown to the largest level first, before a staging buffer
+// is taken or any guest byte is written. A refused growth leaves the image as it is (still
+// GPU-written, tracked and registered, the guest's reads still get it): the caller keeps it for a
+// later pass. Off: true at once, nothing asked for.
+[[nodiscard]] inline bool ReserveEvictionWritebackScratch(Tegra::MemoryManager& gpu_memory,
+                                                          GPUVAddr gpu_addr, const ImageInfo& info,
+                                                          Common::ScratchBuffer<u8>& scratch) {
+    static const bool enabled = Common::PeSwitch("EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM");
+    if (!enabled || info.type == ImageType::Linear ||
+        !WritebackNeedsHeap(gpu_memory, gpu_addr, info, scratch.capacity())) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    const auto sizes = CalculateMipLevelSizes(info);
+    const size_t required = *std::ranges::max_element(sizes);
+    if (required <= scratch.capacity()) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    try {
+        scratch.resize_destructive(required);
+        return true;
+    } catch (const std::bad_alloc&) {
+        // No formatted log while the heap is exhausted: the C stream only.
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM scratch_bytes=%zu\n", required);
+        return false;
+    }
+}
+
 template <class P>
 void TextureCache<P>::RunGarbageCollector() {
     bool high_priority_mode = false;
@@ -615,6 +645,12 @@ void TextureCache<P>::RunGarbageCollector() {
         // EDEN_TEST_GC_WRITEBACK_STARVE=1 (test aid): the write-back below, up to End(), runs in
         // the front's heap wait scope, where EDEN_TEST_HEAP_STARVE_GPU=1 refuses requests.
         GcWritebackStarveScope writeback_starve{must_download && gc_writeback_starve_test};
+        // EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM (above): no scratch for the write-back, the image stays.
+        if (must_download && !ReserveEvictionWritebackScratch(*gpu_memory, image.gpu_addr,
+                                                              image.info, swizzle_data_buffer)) {
+            ++gc_downloads_deferred;
+            return false;
+        }
         if (must_download) {
             // EDEN_UP_GC_ONE_WAIT: this frame's wait is spent; the image stays, nothing is lost.
             if (WaitHeld()) {
@@ -1666,6 +1702,11 @@ void TextureCache<P>::RefusalDownloadEvict() {
     u64 bytes = 0;
     for (const ImageId image_id : chosen) {
         Image& image = slot_images[image_id];
+        // EDEN_NX2_DEFER_WRITEBACK_OOM: this one and the rest stay, as for a refused staging buffer.
+        if (!ReserveEvictionWritebackScratch(*gpu_memory, image.gpu_addr, image.info,
+                                             swizzle_data_buffer)) {
+            break;
+        }
         auto map = TryDownloadStaging(runtime, image.unswizzled_size_bytes, false);
         if (!map) {
             break; // The pool has none: the collector copies the rest back when it gets to them.
